---
export const prerender = true;
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FounderPhoto from '../components/FounderPhoto.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

const CONTACT_EMAIL = SITE.email;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Work with me — TomProtects"
  description="A security advisor for online businesses. What the work is, who it fits, and how to start a conversation."
>
  <Section width="text">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Work with me</h1>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        I'm a security advisor for online businesses. I look at how your business really runs,
        give you a clear plan, and help you make the changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The work covers the same
        <a href="/#how-i-help" class="font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-4"
          >four areas</a
        >
        you'll see on the home page: accounts and access, email and domain, backups and
        continuity, and ongoing security guidance.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Callout on="cream" class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
      <p class="text-ink/80">
        I work with you, not instead of you. We go through the changes together, step by step,
        and you keep the logins. I don't sell products, and I don't take over your accounts.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section background="white" width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Who it fits</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Coaches, consultants, creators, course sellers, and other people running a real business
        online. You might work solo or with a VA, editor, or a few contractors. You don't have an
        IT person, and your revenue depends on things like Stripe, YouTube, Kit, your email, and
        your domain.
      </p>

      <p>I work remotely with online businesses anywhere.</p>

      <p>
        It's worth saying what doesn't fit, too. If you have an in-house IT or security team, want
        a quick tool recommendation instead of a plan, or need a penetration test or a
        certification by a fixed deadline, this isn't the right engagement. I'd rather tell you
        that in the first ten minutes than three weeks in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!--
    Engagement shape. Cream, which is why the booking band below it is now white
    rather than inheriting the page ground — inserting a band here would
    otherwise put two cream bands back to back. The order matters more than the
    grounds: what an engagement costs and how it is shaped belongs before the
    calendar, not after it.
  -->
  <Section width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">How an engagement starts</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Most engagements start with the security foundation assessment. I'll review your setup
        and give you a clear plan, with your priorities in order.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you'd like help, we'll make the changes together on screen-share calls. You stay
        logged in, and you keep your logins.
      </p>

      <p>
        About three months later, we'll do a follow-up review to make sure everything's still in
        place. Until then, you can email me with questions and I'll answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        It covers the things an online business runs on: shared logins, MFA (a second check when
        you sign in) on Stripe, YouTube, your email, and your registrar, domain and email setup,
        account recovery, and backups.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/*
      The one price on the site. A Panel rather than a Callout: a Callout is an
      aside set apart from the copy, and this is not an aside — it is the answer
      to the question a reader arrives with, and it should be findable by
      someone scanning the page rather than reading it.

      font-serif is named explicitly. The base layer only puts Fraunces on
      h1-h4, and this is a <p>: it carries display weight without claiming a
      place in the heading structure, the same way <PullQuote> does.
    */}
    <Card radius="2xl" padding="lg" class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
      <p class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
        Security foundation assessment: starting at $1,000
      </p>
    </Card>

    <h3 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What you get</h3>

    <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-3 text-ink/80">
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> A review of your setup
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> A written plan with your priorities in
        order
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> Hands-on help setting it up, if you
        want it
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> A follow-up review about three months
        later
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> Email help until then
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-ink/70">
      This isn't a penetration test, and I don't monitor your systems.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">
      Why not just buy a one-off audit from a security firm?
    </h3>

    <div class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        It's a reasonable thing to buy, and for some businesses it's the right thing. What it
        usually produces is a report, written to be thorough, and handed over right where the
        actual work starts. If you have someone who can turn a hundred findings into the four
        things worth doing this month, that's a good trade. If you don't, the report becomes one
        more thing nobody has time to read. This isn't a better audit. It's a review that arrives
        already sorted into a plan, with me beside you while you make the changes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section background="white" width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Starting a conversation</h2>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-ink/70">
      A first call is a short conversation about how your business runs online, what's on your
      mind, and whether working together would help. No obligation either way.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex items-center gap-4">
      <FounderPhoto class="h-16 w-16 shrink-0" />
      <p class="text-sm text-ink/70">
        You'll be talking to me directly. I'm Tom, with CISSP, CISM, CDPSE and PMP certifications
        and more than twenty years in security.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-3 text-ink/80">
      {/*
        Deliberately no duration here. The calendar below is booked as a
        30-minute slot, and the v1 copy this page replaced said 15–20, so the
        two contradicted each other on the same screen. Whichever is right, it
        belongs in TidyCal — stating it twice only means it can drift again.
      */}
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> A short call, no pressure, no pitch
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> Plain-English answers to your security
        questions
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> A clear sense of whether an engagement
        would help, and what it would involve
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- TidyCal scheduling embed — unchanged: same account, same path, same markup. -->
    <Card radius="2xl" padding="xs" surface="cream" class="mt-10 sm:p-4">
      <div class="tidycal-embed" data-path="tomsmith/discovery"></div>
      <script is:inline src="https://asset-tidycal.b-cdn.net/js/embed.js" async></script>
      <noscript>
        <p class="p-4 text-ink/70">
          The booking calendar needs JavaScript. You can also book directly at
          <a href="https://tidycal.com/tomsmith/discovery" class="font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-4">tidycal.com/tomsmith/discovery</a>.
        </p>
      </noscript>
    </Card>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-sm text-ink/70">
      Would rather write than book a slot? Email
      <a
        href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
        class="font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-4">{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
      > and tell me where things stand.
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
